1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:02,140
And now, to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,200
Did you know I was a master campfire cook?

3
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:13,100
In my own mind?

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:15,720
Here I am, I'm a Weebelos den leader.

5
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:17,680
I have 18 boys in my Weebelos den.

6
00:00:18,220 --> 00:00:19,760
And we're making pancakes one morning.

7
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:21,260
Basically not we, but me.

8
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:22,800
I'm making pancakes one morning.

9
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:26,060
And I'm slinging those things out of the frying pan as fast as I can,

10
00:00:26,180 --> 00:00:27,460
because the boys are ravenous.

11
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:32,299
And, of course, one or two of them get out of the pan without being cooked all the way through.

12
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:35,800
And I'm thinking, well, hopefully nobody will notice.

13
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:36,940
But one of the boys did.

14
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:39,380
He noticed that I didn't cook the pancake all the way through.

15
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:41,460
Wasn't displeased.

16
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:42,080
No.

17
00:00:42,460 --> 00:00:47,160
Came back to me and he said, hey, do you have any more with the cheese in the middle?

18
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:48,360
Oh, my.

19
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:50,019
Oh, my.

20
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:53,960
Oh, this is podcast number 16.

21
00:00:57,460 --> 00:00:59,460
Hey.

22
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:01,060
Yeah.

23
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:01,360
Hey.

24
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:17,980
Yeah.

25
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:18,100
Yeah.

26
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:22,500
Yeah.

27
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:28,320
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

28
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:29,520
This is Clark Green.

29
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:32,640
In Scoutmaster's ship in seven minutes or less,

30
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:35,440
which I've got to be honest with you, is more than seven minutes.

31
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:39,700
It's more like ten if you've got a stopwatch on you right now.

32
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:45,060
We're going to be answering an email from Scoutmaster in Minnesota.

33
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:46,000
His name is Larry.

34
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:51,820
He wrote in frustration over his patrol leader council's kind of inability

35
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:57,539
to make good plans, and there's sometimes lack of imagination in planning.

36
00:01:58,619 --> 00:01:59,919
Sounds familiar, doesn't it?

37
00:02:01,580 --> 00:02:05,660
Next, in a great brotherhood of service,

38
00:02:05,839 --> 00:02:08,039
which is kind of a new feature I'm hoping will work,

39
00:02:08,359 --> 00:02:11,120
is an interview with a Scout in Tunisia.

40
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:13,920
That's right, Northern Africa in Tunisia.

41
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:17,900
And hopefully this will be an ongoing series on the podcast

42
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:20,579
where we talk with Scouts around the world.

43
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:22,020
Thought it would be interesting.

44
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:23,880
I'm certainly interested in it.

45
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:24,420
I hope you are.

46
00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:26,460
That's about it for this time.

47
00:02:27,260 --> 00:02:28,120
So let's get started.

48
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:33,360
Scoutmaster ship in seven minutes or less.

49
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:43,980
Here's an email that came from a Scoutmaster in Minnesota.

50
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:44,900
His name is Larry.

51
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:45,580
He said,

52
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:49,700
I just recently came across your podcast and had to let you know that I love what I'm hearing.

53
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:50,480
Well, thanks, Larry.

54
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:52,500
It's always good to hear back from folks.

55
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:53,500
He says,

56
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:57,380
I've been involved with a troop in Minnesota for about 12 years now

57
00:02:57,380 --> 00:03:00,040
and took over as Scoutmaster not quite two years ago.

58
00:03:00,460 --> 00:03:03,440
I've been trying to take as much info from others as I can.

59
00:03:04,120 --> 00:03:08,600
Our last Scoutmaster pretty much did everything on his own for the boys and for the committee.

60
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:10,640
Well, that's kind of a familiar story.

61
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:17,100
When I was asked to take the Scoutmaster position, I told the committee chairman we were going to make this troop boy run

62
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:19,420
and the committee was going to act as a committee.

63
00:03:19,519 --> 00:03:21,519
Well, those are good steps in the right direction, Larry.

64
00:03:21,959 --> 00:03:26,679
He has been doing a great job of getting more parents involved and slowly building up a real committee.

65
00:03:26,839 --> 00:03:30,359
And that's a slow process sometimes, but good for you guys.

66
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:31,320
You're on the right track.

67
00:03:31,799 --> 00:03:33,019
He goes on to say this.

68
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:37,000
I, for my part, have been having a heck of a time getting the boys to do their part.

69
00:03:37,860 --> 00:03:41,400
My biggest frustration has been their lack of imagination and planning.

70
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:47,060
I try to remain silent at the Patrol Leaders' Council meetings, but nothing seems to get done.

71
00:03:47,440 --> 00:03:51,880
I'm feeling like we went so many years of them not doing any actual planning

72
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:54,800
that none of them have a clue what they should be doing.

73
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:59,500
So I'm wondering if you have any tips or tricks to spur the Patrol Leaders' Council on

74
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:01,840
and actually put together a good program.

75
00:04:01,840 --> 00:04:02,920
Now, don't get me wrong.

76
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:04,880
I'm not saying we have a lousy program.

77
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:08,680
It's just pretty much the same thing year after year, month after month.

78
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:12,800
Thought I would just bounce this off of you and see what advice you might have for me.

79
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:15,520
So, Larry, here's my advice.

80
00:04:15,720 --> 00:04:20,760
I really understand your frustration with scouts being somewhat unimaginative

81
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:23,220
or being kind of lackadaisical planners.

82
00:04:23,860 --> 00:04:26,300
I know this is the way my scouts often appear to me.

83
00:04:26,880 --> 00:04:28,240
So let me ask you this question.

84
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:32,040
Could this be more a matter of perspective than anything else?

85
00:04:32,460 --> 00:04:36,760
You say you have a good program, but it's the same from year to year and month to month.

86
00:04:37,060 --> 00:04:42,500
Now, it may be that your scouts really like the activities they've done over and over again.

87
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:47,060
Perhaps they might assume or think that they're expected to do the same activities.

88
00:04:48,860 --> 00:04:50,880
Here's something that drives me crazy.

89
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:57,580
My scouts have set up the same day trip to the same climbing gym every December for the past eight or ten years.

90
00:04:58,060 --> 00:05:00,659
I have absolutely no interest in it personally.

91
00:05:01,380 --> 00:05:05,000
I always send other adults to cover for me on that particular trip.

92
00:05:05,100 --> 00:05:06,780
I get busy around that time of year anyway.

93
00:05:07,620 --> 00:05:10,000
I try to dissuade them from doing this.

94
00:05:10,380 --> 00:05:13,440
When they have their patrol leader's council meeting, they're setting up a calendar.

95
00:05:13,960 --> 00:05:15,400
They talk about this climbing trip.

96
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:19,320
And I say, geez, guys, you've been going there for a long time.

97
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:20,719
Are you sure that's what you want to do?

98
00:05:21,219 --> 00:05:24,099
And they assure me, yes, that's what we really want to do.

99
00:05:24,719 --> 00:05:26,020
And I said, well, all right.

100
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:31,980
And, you know, I've tried everything short of forbidding them to go, but I can't change their minds.

101
00:05:32,060 --> 00:05:36,280
I mean, they really love to go to this climbing gym year after ever, love a year.

102
00:05:36,960 --> 00:05:43,380
So I know the frustration sometimes of parts of a program that have been going on for a long time

103
00:05:43,380 --> 00:05:49,360
and that the scouts really enjoy, but bore a scoutmaster to tears.

104
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:56,780
What I've come to realize is that my scouts, like most scouts I know, really enjoy familiarity

105
00:05:56,780 --> 00:06:00,760
and predictability of some troop traditions.

106
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:04,700
They're not yearning for wildly innovative changes.

107
00:06:05,260 --> 00:06:07,020
I mean, they like what they've come to know.

108
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:12,159
And I have to keep reminding myself that the things I've seen and done again over and over

109
00:06:12,159 --> 00:06:16,560
for the past 26 years are they are experiencing for the first time.

110
00:06:17,599 --> 00:06:23,360
If the troop program did not change at all from year to year, the scouts would see it a maximum

111
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:24,900
of what, six or seven times?

112
00:06:24,900 --> 00:06:29,300
If they come in and they're 10 and a half or 11 and they age out at around 18.

113
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:31,620
Now the first time they see it, it's totally new.

114
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:36,360
Maybe the second time they see it, it's still pretty new and fun.

115
00:06:37,340 --> 00:06:44,320
And then maybe the third and fourth time they see it, now they're beginning to get really

116
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:45,800
familiar with it and understand it.

117
00:06:46,680 --> 00:06:48,760
And they start to get good at it.

118
00:06:49,180 --> 00:06:53,720
And then finally, you know, in the last couple of times they're going, well, they're leading

119
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:55,240
the other scouts through it.

120
00:06:55,240 --> 00:07:05,800
So even if a scout has seen the same weekend, the same activity five or six times, it changes

121
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:07,800
for him over that length of time.

122
00:07:07,920 --> 00:07:12,980
I mean, he's getting older and wiser and he's developing leadership skills.

123
00:07:13,420 --> 00:07:18,820
And so he's going to look at it a little bit differently than I will, who has seen it,

124
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:23,540
you know, basically as an adult for, oh geez, you know, 10 or 12 times easily.

125
00:07:23,540 --> 00:07:31,160
Now, moving on to planning and their apparent lack of planning.

126
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:36,300
I got to tell you, what I consider to be a detailed comprehensive plan and what my scouts

127
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:40,260
see as a detailed comprehensive plan are two different animals altogether.

128
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:43,720
Most of the patrol leaders council meetings end.

129
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:50,120
I don't sit in the room with them because I can't, because I can't keep my mouth shut.

130
00:07:50,120 --> 00:07:52,120
Larry, maybe you're having the same problem.

131
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:53,720
It sounds like it.

132
00:07:54,300 --> 00:07:54,660
Okay.

133
00:07:54,860 --> 00:07:56,000
Not a personal failing.

134
00:07:56,280 --> 00:07:58,560
It takes a bit of an ego to be a scout master.

135
00:07:59,340 --> 00:08:02,940
And, uh, you know, you can see more than they can see you.

136
00:08:03,060 --> 00:08:06,700
You're, you're older and more experienced and boy, you want to jump in every time.

137
00:08:07,080 --> 00:08:09,960
So what I do, I walk out of the room.

138
00:08:10,060 --> 00:08:11,840
I let them have their patrol leaders council.

139
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:17,320
And, uh, they think, uh, planning is done when they've just acknowledged the fact that

140
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:18,700
they've planned something.

141
00:08:19,419 --> 00:08:22,239
It, here's what happens.

142
00:08:22,859 --> 00:08:24,780
They say that we're, oh, we're going to go canoeing.

143
00:08:24,840 --> 00:08:25,000
Okay.

144
00:08:25,020 --> 00:08:25,419
That's great.

145
00:08:25,479 --> 00:08:26,239
Where are we going canoeing?

146
00:08:26,280 --> 00:08:27,640
Oh, uh, over on this river.

147
00:08:27,799 --> 00:08:29,020
Oh, that'll be great.

148
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:30,059
How long is it going to take?

149
00:08:30,120 --> 00:08:30,760
Oh, I don't know.

150
00:08:32,100 --> 00:08:36,339
I mean, they, they have a very different consideration of a depth of planning.

151
00:08:36,340 --> 00:08:43,140
Now, what I have to do is I'll sit down with the senior patrol leader and the patrol leaders

152
00:08:43,140 --> 00:08:47,980
council after they've finished with their deliberations and I'll say, okay, fellows,

153
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:49,280
so tell me about your plans.

154
00:08:49,520 --> 00:08:53,040
They'll tell me about the plans and there'll be giant holes that you could drive a truck

155
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:54,120
through in their plans.

156
00:08:54,240 --> 00:08:57,760
I mean, you know, they, they will not have thought of some very basic things.

157
00:08:58,400 --> 00:09:04,260
Um, and, uh, so I will say, okay, fellows, what you need to do then is let me ask you these

158
00:09:04,260 --> 00:09:04,740
questions.

159
00:09:04,940 --> 00:09:06,200
How is this going to happen?

160
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:07,240
How is that going to happen?

161
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:08,800
Who's going to be responsible for this?

162
00:09:08,960 --> 00:09:12,320
And they'll kind of stare at me like a dog with a new pan most of the time.

163
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:14,420
And they'll say, oh, well, we really didn't talk about that.

164
00:09:14,480 --> 00:09:16,420
I said, okay, well, you still have some time.

165
00:09:17,080 --> 00:09:19,440
Uh, let me leave you to working on this.

166
00:09:19,540 --> 00:09:23,480
Do you need, do you need any advice about any of these things where there's things you

167
00:09:23,480 --> 00:09:25,120
weren't certain about or something like that?

168
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:31,020
And, you know, I can, I can kind of help them fill in the blanks, uh, by letting them ask

169
00:09:31,020 --> 00:09:32,160
those questions of me.

170
00:09:32,160 --> 00:09:33,760
And I asked some questions of them.

171
00:09:33,760 --> 00:09:39,360
And so it's not, uh, dictatorial or, or, uh, me sitting there and giving them a set of

172
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:39,740
directions.

173
00:09:39,740 --> 00:09:41,819
It's kind of helping them discover what they missed.

174
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:45,319
And then they'll fill in the blanks and they'll come up with a reasonably good plan.

175
00:09:45,320 --> 00:09:49,300
And I'll be quizzing my senior patrol leader about that as the event goes closer, just

176
00:09:49,300 --> 00:09:50,840
to make sure they have a handle on it.

177
00:09:51,280 --> 00:09:56,580
Now, Larry, I think you got a very legitimate point is that you say you've been the new scout

178
00:09:56,580 --> 00:10:01,380
master for just over a year, maybe, maybe close to two years.

179
00:10:01,380 --> 00:10:07,720
That's, that's, that's a relatively short amount of time for boys to begin to catch on to this

180
00:10:07,720 --> 00:10:08,220
new idea.

181
00:10:08,220 --> 00:10:13,320
Because, you know, if the old scout master did everything, did all the planning and just

182
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:20,060
kind of gave a presented the program to the boys, like a tour guide, then, you know, you've

183
00:10:20,060 --> 00:10:21,240
got a bit of a hill to climb.

184
00:10:21,420 --> 00:10:26,980
They don't understand and they don't know, and they don't have anything to base, uh, those

185
00:10:26,980 --> 00:10:28,020
expectations on.

186
00:10:28,020 --> 00:10:31,380
And so what we need to do is we need to introduce a little bit of training and I'm going to suggest

187
00:10:31,380 --> 00:10:31,840
a method.

188
00:10:32,040 --> 00:10:37,439
Take five or 10 minutes at your patrol leaders, council meetings, either before or after, and

189
00:10:37,439 --> 00:10:41,760
introduce them to some broad concepts that will help them be better planners.

190
00:10:42,540 --> 00:10:48,079
I'm not a big fan of sending them all away to a big event, you know, to get trained or

191
00:10:48,079 --> 00:10:51,860
setting up an event to get trained because boys are kind of suspicious of this stuff.

192
00:10:52,300 --> 00:10:56,400
And they know that they're just kind of pretending to do something when they're at a training

193
00:10:56,400 --> 00:11:01,199
event, but when they're actually doing something, you give them the information just prior to

194
00:11:01,199 --> 00:11:01,880
they're doing that.

195
00:11:02,280 --> 00:11:03,819
And they actually set up a plan.

196
00:11:04,020 --> 00:11:05,020
They're going to be much happier.

197
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:06,680
Now, this is the way it would work for me.

198
00:11:07,280 --> 00:11:11,000
I would ask my senior patrol leader to have the first five minutes of the patrol leaders

199
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:11,600
council meeting.

200
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:16,060
And I would say, okay, let's talk about what constitutes a pretty thorough plan.

201
00:11:16,280 --> 00:11:19,079
And we'll go around and we'll do a little back and forth.

202
00:11:19,120 --> 00:11:22,680
And I'll have a set of points that I want to make, but I'll try and draw it out of them

203
00:11:22,680 --> 00:11:25,459
rather than just stand there and preach it to them.

204
00:11:25,880 --> 00:11:28,339
They'll say, well, we actually have to know this stuff.

205
00:11:28,680 --> 00:11:28,880
Huh?

206
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:31,640
Well, that would make life a lot easier.

207
00:11:32,140 --> 00:11:37,140
They'll get it just by working with them piece by piece, introducing a little something

208
00:11:37,140 --> 00:11:39,120
new at every patrol leaders council meeting.

209
00:11:40,219 --> 00:11:43,380
Soon they will begin to work right along with you.

210
00:11:43,380 --> 00:11:48,800
I will say this from my personal experience, and I don't see this in your email area, and

211
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:54,479
I'm not trying to intend anything upon you, but for everyone, this is really frustrating.

212
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:59,459
When the boys don't do what you're expecting them to do, it is really frustrating.

213
00:11:59,579 --> 00:12:00,699
And it can tend towards anger.

214
00:12:00,859 --> 00:12:06,640
And I can remember in the deep distant past being angry with the boys about not formulating

215
00:12:06,640 --> 00:12:07,300
a good plan.

216
00:12:07,719 --> 00:12:11,400
It wasn't something that they were capable of doing and they were refusing to do.

217
00:12:11,560 --> 00:12:13,380
It was something they really didn't understand.

218
00:12:13,380 --> 00:12:21,660
Now, once I realized that they weren't just being willful, but they just didn't know what

219
00:12:21,660 --> 00:12:28,380
to do, I came down off of Mount Olympus where I would stand, you know, empirically and say,

220
00:12:28,560 --> 00:12:34,220
I demand you to submit to me the sacrifice of a fully formed plan.

221
00:12:34,820 --> 00:12:38,020
And if they didn't do that, well, watch out for the thunderbolts, right?

222
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:43,540
Instead of that, I got on their side and tried to help them understand the process.

223
00:12:44,340 --> 00:12:49,980
They wanted to plan things well because they knew it would enhance their experience in scouting

224
00:12:49,980 --> 00:12:56,319
rather than planning it because otherwise I would just kind of get angry with them.

225
00:12:57,020 --> 00:12:58,520
You know, you're on the right track.

226
00:12:58,860 --> 00:13:00,380
You'll build these things over time.

227
00:13:00,699 --> 00:13:06,740
I would say two or three years is a reasonable amount of time to start to build an expectation

228
00:13:06,740 --> 00:13:07,660
in a group of scouts.

229
00:13:08,320 --> 00:13:10,060
I think you'll have a wonderful time with it.

230
00:13:10,060 --> 00:13:14,640
Thanks again, Larry, for writing and I hope the information helps you.

231
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:39,580
The scout movement is forming a personal tie between the different foreign countries, a living force,

232
00:13:39,580 --> 00:13:43,540
a great brotherhood of service, a joyous work.

233
00:13:48,100 --> 00:13:55,200
That's the voice of our founder, Lord Robert Baden-Powell, under whose leadership scouting spread around the world

234
00:13:55,200 --> 00:14:00,380
and became, as he described, a living force, a great brotherhood of service, and a joyous work.

235
00:14:00,900 --> 00:14:05,240
This marks a new feature on the podcast where we hope to speak with scouters from all over the world

236
00:14:05,240 --> 00:14:10,320
and understand a little bit about what scouting is there and all the wonderful things that we have in common.

237
00:14:11,460 --> 00:14:14,320
This time we're going to be talking with Mohamed Faswi-Jemma,

238
00:14:14,620 --> 00:14:20,320
and he lives in Tunisia, in the capital of Tunisia, which is in northern Africa.

239
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:27,000
And if you look on the Mediterranean at northern Africa, soon you'll find Tunisia,

240
00:14:27,100 --> 00:14:31,500
which is about the size in square mileage of Wisconsin or Florida.

241
00:14:32,440 --> 00:14:40,160
They have 28,000 scouts in the Scouts of Tunisia, who was founded in the 1930s.

242
00:14:40,160 --> 00:14:46,020
And Mohamed Faswi-Jemma is a scouter there, a trainer.

243
00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:52,180
He's worked on several international projects with the World Scouting Organization

244
00:14:52,180 --> 00:14:58,240
and is currently working on a kind of a link that's going to result in a summer program

245
00:14:58,240 --> 00:15:00,780
between the Scouts of Tunisia and the Scouts of Denmark.

246
00:15:01,079 --> 00:15:02,680
Very interesting fellow to talk with.

247
00:15:02,939 --> 00:15:06,000
He worked at Philmont here in the States for a season.

248
00:15:06,400 --> 00:15:08,100
Here's the discussion that we had.

249
00:15:08,100 --> 00:15:10,100
Hey, Clark.

250
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,080
So I'm very happy to meet you.

251
00:15:13,520 --> 00:15:18,740
You know, I have been in America, and I work for Boy Scouts of America in Philmont.

252
00:15:19,140 --> 00:15:21,820
And it was a great experience for me.

253
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:23,440
You know, I was working in services.

254
00:15:23,740 --> 00:15:31,040
It is a place where we give food and stuff and equipment and packs and gas for the common crews.

255
00:15:31,420 --> 00:15:31,600
Right.

256
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:36,500
So I had the opportunity to meet most of the Scouts coming to Philmont.

257
00:15:36,500 --> 00:15:38,820
So it was a great experience.

258
00:15:39,620 --> 00:15:47,660
And every day I gave about 20 or 25 speeches to each crew.

259
00:15:48,160 --> 00:15:48,520
Uh-huh.

260
00:15:48,600 --> 00:15:50,120
So it was a lot for me.

261
00:15:50,360 --> 00:15:54,380
But it improves my language skills, you know.

262
00:15:54,480 --> 00:15:56,540
That's why I'm English like that, you know.

263
00:15:56,740 --> 00:15:59,700
Tell me about being a scout in Tunisia.

264
00:15:59,700 --> 00:16:02,600
I think I was in the second year in high school.

265
00:16:02,700 --> 00:16:05,780
And I had a friend studying with me in the same class.

266
00:16:06,340 --> 00:16:11,000
He asked me if I could participate with them in a spring camp.

267
00:16:11,380 --> 00:16:11,700
Uh-huh.

268
00:16:11,860 --> 00:16:13,020
And I accepted.

269
00:16:13,460 --> 00:16:14,640
And it was my first time.

270
00:16:15,260 --> 00:16:15,760
Oh, nice.

271
00:16:15,760 --> 00:16:21,200
Was scouting in the United States different from Tunisia?

272
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:23,860
I don't think so.

273
00:16:23,939 --> 00:16:25,439
We're not so different, you know.

274
00:16:25,660 --> 00:16:33,340
Because both of United States scouts and Tunisian scouts, we have the same objectives, the same things to work on, you know.

275
00:16:33,340 --> 00:16:41,700
Maybe there's a little bit difference between something like administration, like some kinds of activities.

276
00:16:42,400 --> 00:16:46,580
But still, there's not a gap, a big gap between us, you know.

277
00:16:46,640 --> 00:16:48,260
There's not a big difference between us.

278
00:16:48,660 --> 00:16:54,740
Well, in the United States, of course, with the Boy Scouts of America, the scout motto is be prepared.

279
00:16:55,380 --> 00:16:59,280
So in Arabic, how do you say be prepared?

280
00:16:59,280 --> 00:17:01,760
In Tunisia, we say it's kun musta'eddan.

281
00:17:01,959 --> 00:17:03,339
B, it's kun.

282
00:17:03,439 --> 00:17:03,720
Kun.

283
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:04,560
Prepared.

284
00:17:05,060 --> 00:17:05,579
Musta'eddan.

285
00:17:06,139 --> 00:17:06,740
Musta'eddan?

286
00:17:06,760 --> 00:17:07,780
Yes, kun musta'eddan.

287
00:17:08,680 --> 00:17:10,160
Kun musta'eddan.

288
00:17:10,379 --> 00:17:10,680
Okay.

289
00:17:10,839 --> 00:17:12,280
It's a little bit difficult to pronounce.

290
00:17:13,359 --> 00:17:17,560
It has letters that we don't have over here.

291
00:17:18,099 --> 00:17:20,819
Yeah, like the A.

292
00:17:21,020 --> 00:17:21,339
Yeah.

293
00:17:21,500 --> 00:17:27,180
Now, I'm a project manager in a corporation between Scouts of Tunisia and Danish Scouts.

294
00:17:27,180 --> 00:17:30,080
And we will have a camp together.

295
00:17:30,440 --> 00:17:31,820
And when will this event be?

296
00:17:31,960 --> 00:17:32,760
In July.

297
00:17:33,260 --> 00:17:33,580
Okay.

298
00:17:33,800 --> 00:17:37,000
So this is like a summer camp for Scouts?

299
00:17:37,360 --> 00:17:39,680
Yeah, it's like summer camp, yeah, for Scouts.

300
00:17:39,840 --> 00:17:42,680
Between 12 and 16 years old, something like that.

301
00:17:42,680 --> 00:17:46,260
This type of thing is pretty common over in that part of the world, isn't it?

302
00:17:46,480 --> 00:17:49,880
Getting together with Scouts from other countries and things like that.

303
00:17:50,040 --> 00:17:51,460
It's so rare here.

304
00:17:51,460 --> 00:18:01,820
I believe in that because when I was working in Philmont, every time I made a speech for a crew, when I told them that I'm from Tunisia, they were starting to look at them.

305
00:18:01,940 --> 00:18:04,560
Wow, you're coming from North Africa.

306
00:18:05,720 --> 00:18:07,140
They could not believe.

307
00:18:07,140 --> 00:18:12,600
Some of them told me that this is the first time we met someone coming from North Africa.

308
00:18:13,240 --> 00:18:14,180
Yeah, yeah.

309
00:18:14,380 --> 00:18:16,160
It's a big deal.

310
00:18:16,420 --> 00:18:18,320
How did you feel when you went to Philmont?

311
00:18:18,460 --> 00:18:24,480
Did you feel as though people were respectful and welcoming and they were supportive?

312
00:18:25,320 --> 00:18:26,900
They were completely supportive.

313
00:18:27,640 --> 00:18:28,520
And guess what?

314
00:18:28,680 --> 00:18:28,840
Yeah.

315
00:18:28,840 --> 00:18:32,959
My close friend was a Jew.

316
00:18:33,439 --> 00:18:38,159
So I think that there's no problem between religions, believe me.

317
00:18:38,939 --> 00:18:45,280
I believe that Muslims and Jews and Christian people, they can be very close friends.

318
00:18:45,720 --> 00:18:48,679
I think that Scout, the Scout is very open-minded.

319
00:18:48,679 --> 00:18:49,740
I think so too.

320
00:18:50,179 --> 00:18:57,459
You know, the people I have encountered, and it's admittedly few from around the world who have been involved in Scouting, all have the same idea.

321
00:18:57,460 --> 00:19:05,560
And even though we may have differences, the differences are very small compared to the larger idea of Scouting.

322
00:19:05,840 --> 00:19:14,760
That's why I believe that Jamborees, World Jamborees, are very good because it is an opportunity that Scouts from all over the world.

323
00:19:14,760 --> 00:19:23,720
They meet each other and exchange experiences, exchange games, exchange songs, activities, things like that.

324
00:19:23,720 --> 00:19:30,540
So what's the general experience of a young person in Scouts in Tunisia?

325
00:19:30,680 --> 00:19:35,140
Now, do you have Boy Scouts and Girl Scouts or are they together?

326
00:19:35,260 --> 00:19:35,920
They are together.

327
00:19:36,260 --> 00:19:36,540
Okay.

328
00:19:36,680 --> 00:19:40,660
So if I'm a Boy Scout in Tunisia, there would be girls in my Scout troop?

329
00:19:40,660 --> 00:19:40,880
Yes.

330
00:19:40,880 --> 00:19:51,000
No, I mean, you will find a Scout girls troop, a Boy Scout troop, but most of the time you do activities together.

331
00:19:51,420 --> 00:19:51,700
Okay.

332
00:19:51,740 --> 00:19:54,460
You camp together and do activities together.

333
00:19:54,900 --> 00:19:59,620
And so you have explorers or rovers for older Scouts?

334
00:20:00,140 --> 00:20:00,480
Yes.

335
00:20:01,060 --> 00:20:01,920
Yeah, yeah, yeah, we have.

336
00:20:02,040 --> 00:20:02,380
Yeah, we have.

337
00:20:02,660 --> 00:20:06,060
Normally, rovers start from the age of 18.

338
00:20:06,540 --> 00:20:06,800
Yeah.

339
00:20:07,220 --> 00:20:07,640
Yeah.

340
00:20:07,640 --> 00:20:09,640
And that goes up through…

341
00:20:10,420 --> 00:20:11,140
It depends.

342
00:20:11,240 --> 00:20:11,760
It depends.

343
00:20:12,240 --> 00:20:20,300
Because at the age of 19, you can start the training courses to be Scout master.

344
00:20:20,760 --> 00:20:21,100
Right.

345
00:20:21,460 --> 00:20:25,480
So you can just spend one year in rovers.

346
00:20:26,140 --> 00:20:29,620
And if you choose, it depends on the boy or the girl.

347
00:20:29,620 --> 00:20:34,879
And if she or he chooses, he can spend four years, five years.

348
00:20:35,060 --> 00:20:38,959
Then after that, he can start the training courses to be a Scout master.

349
00:20:39,699 --> 00:20:42,000
How big is a troop in Tunisia?

350
00:20:42,080 --> 00:20:43,199
I imagine there's all sizes.

351
00:20:43,379 --> 00:20:44,939
But what would be a large group?

352
00:20:45,840 --> 00:20:47,439
You know, there's a rule normally.

353
00:20:47,600 --> 00:20:53,540
Normally, for a Scout troop, for example, it has not to exceed more than 32 Scouts, Boy Scouts.

354
00:20:53,620 --> 00:20:54,060
Ah, okay.

355
00:20:54,060 --> 00:20:58,120
So we divide them into four crews.

356
00:20:58,620 --> 00:21:01,360
Each crew contains eight Scouts.

357
00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:04,419
So eight times four, it will be 32.

358
00:21:05,379 --> 00:21:07,980
And normally, it has four Scout masters.

359
00:21:08,460 --> 00:21:10,000
What do they do during the year?

360
00:21:10,120 --> 00:21:13,220
Do they go camping and things like that?

361
00:21:13,280 --> 00:21:16,620
We have internal activities in our club.

362
00:21:17,060 --> 00:21:19,580
You know, we have a building that we call our club.

363
00:21:19,960 --> 00:21:23,379
We have internal activities and we have external activities.

364
00:21:23,380 --> 00:21:26,400
So we met, normally we met every weekend.

365
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:30,160
And it depends on our plan and our program.

366
00:21:30,320 --> 00:21:34,460
So sometimes we have activities in our club.

367
00:21:34,960 --> 00:21:40,880
Something like meetings, like singing, like games, like doing some activities, you know.

368
00:21:41,500 --> 00:21:46,780
And sometimes we go outside to the forest, you know, maybe to meet other troops.

369
00:21:46,780 --> 00:21:52,399
Maybe to make a research about the nature, about animals, about insects, things like that.

370
00:21:52,940 --> 00:21:53,580
Things like that.

371
00:21:54,240 --> 00:21:54,320
Yeah.

372
00:21:54,520 --> 00:22:01,780
But we have, which is normal in Tunisia, we have a winter camp, which is in December.

373
00:22:02,639 --> 00:22:03,100
Okay.

374
00:22:03,200 --> 00:22:05,379
We have a spring camp, which is in March.

375
00:22:05,960 --> 00:22:06,440
Yes.

376
00:22:06,440 --> 00:22:07,900
And we have the summer camp.

377
00:22:07,900 --> 00:22:11,320
It can be in June or July or August.

378
00:22:11,720 --> 00:22:12,720
We call to 11.

379
00:22:13,760 --> 00:22:17,320
And tell me again, tell me again, be prepared in Arabic.

380
00:22:17,980 --> 00:22:19,040
Kun musta'eddan.

381
00:22:19,380 --> 00:22:21,860
You don't have to, you don't have to pronounce it da'a.

382
00:22:21,980 --> 00:22:23,660
So just write it.

383
00:22:23,880 --> 00:22:24,080
Kun.

384
00:22:24,220 --> 00:22:24,540
Kun.

385
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:26,560
Musta'eddan.

386
00:22:26,940 --> 00:22:27,380
Musta.

387
00:22:27,520 --> 00:22:28,080
Musta.

388
00:22:28,620 --> 00:22:29,140
Musta'eddan.

389
00:22:29,200 --> 00:22:29,940
Musta'eddan.

390
00:22:30,880 --> 00:22:31,100
Yeah.

391
00:22:31,260 --> 00:22:32,000
Kun musta'eddan.

392
00:22:32,120 --> 00:22:33,920
Well, it was very, very nice to meet you.

393
00:22:34,260 --> 00:22:34,480
Right.

394
00:22:34,600 --> 00:22:34,800
Okay.

395
00:22:35,080 --> 00:22:35,800
See you later.

396
00:22:36,380 --> 00:22:39,120
And have a good time and say hi to your scouts.

